WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.096
EvoViM starts with a question about memory.

00:00:03.296 --> 00:00:06.845
Imagine your only job is to
reach the end of this gallery.

00:00:07.045 --> 00:00:09.994
At the exit, someone shows you a painting.

00:00:10.194 --> 00:00:12.121
Which door did it hang beside?

00:00:12.321 --> 00:00:15.850
Would you have saved the painting,
the door number, or both?

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.538
In DMV-Hard, a tiny patch has to lead
back to one of a hundred product pages.

00:00:22.738 --> 00:00:25.849
The gallery asks for a door and a side.

00:00:26.049 --> 00:00:29.811
Poster Maze asks which wall
in which cell held a poster.

00:00:30.011 --> 00:00:33.052
All three reveal their
questions after exploration.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.216
Other tasks tell you the goal immediately.

00:00:36.416 --> 00:00:39.992
In Tetris, find a landing
for the falling piece.

00:00:40.192 --> 00:00:43.965
In Pac-Man, reach the pellets
while avoiding ghosts.

00:00:44.165 --> 00:00:48.658
In Card Matching, remember where a face
appeared so you can flip its partner.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.004
First, AutoMem evolves
a text memory program.

00:00:53.204 --> 00:00:56.984
It described both swatches
as teal with rust and navy

00:00:56.984 --> 00:01:00.472
spots, then picked the
table instead of the vase.

00:01:00.672 --> 00:01:05.049
Haiku identified just one of a
hundred and six generated patterns.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.427
Second, a hand-built
memory saves every frame.

00:01:09.627 --> 00:01:14.330
Adding a pixel matcher found the right
painting in fifty-six of sixty cases.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.153
How many locations did Haiku get right?

00:01:17.353 --> 00:01:17.945
Zero.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.188
Third, write the program
before entering the gallery.

00:01:22.388 --> 00:01:25.337
It dutifully logged the order of the doors.

00:01:25.537 --> 00:01:29.484
When the question arrived,
the paintings were missing from its records.

00:01:30.273 --> 00:01:32.421
The pilots showed what gets lost.

00:01:32.621 --> 00:01:35.773
In EvoViM, one frozen model revises how its

00:01:35.773 --> 00:01:38.705
memory program stores and uses evidence.

00:01:38.905 --> 00:01:41.262
The model acts on its first observation.

00:01:41.462 --> 00:01:46.175
After each action, Record can save the
next view, like this one at door seven.

00:01:46.375 --> 00:01:50.276
Pixel computation compares the
query painting with frame memory.

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:53.541
Visual recall brings matches
into the next prompt.

00:01:53.741 --> 00:01:57.584
At Act, the model answers: door seven, left.

00:01:58.373 --> 00:02:01.659
Memory evolution starts
from development episodes.

00:02:01.859 --> 00:02:05.342
Enhanced feedback helps trace
missing evidence to a tool call.

00:02:05.542 --> 00:02:09.478
The same model revises prompts,
memory operations, or tools.

00:02:09.678 --> 00:02:12.197
It also rewrites its revision experience.

00:02:12.397 --> 00:02:17.784
Notes preserve findings, the playbook
guides diagnosis, and scripts rerun checks.

00:02:17.984 --> 00:02:21.943
After code checks, the candidate
runs on the same seeds.

00:02:22.143 --> 00:02:23.978
The gate selects the next parent.

00:02:24.178 --> 00:02:28.241
Revision experience survives rejection
and guides the next revision.

00:02:28.441 --> 00:02:30.287
This makes evolution recursive.

00:02:31.073 --> 00:02:35.810
In DMV-Hard, offline matcher edits
failed to improve recognition.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.088
The model logged that in its playbook.

00:02:38.288 --> 00:02:39.716
A later revision read it.

00:02:39.916 --> 00:02:42.238
Retrieval usually found the right page.

00:02:42.438 --> 00:02:45.782
It added a tool to compare image
regions at the same scale.

00:02:45.982 --> 00:02:48.002
Here it is on an unseen episode.

00:02:48.807 --> 00:02:54.403
On new episodes, EvoViM leads the
six-task average with all three models.

00:02:54.603 --> 00:02:57.710
Its lead over AutoMem ranges from thirty-one

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.535
to nearly forty-eight percentage points.

00:03:00.735 --> 00:03:02.523
Individual tasks differ.

00:03:02.723 --> 00:03:06.984
With GPT in Poster Maze,
AutoMem still leads.

00:03:07.773 --> 00:03:09.968
Watch one gallery program evolve.

00:03:10.168 --> 00:03:14.870
Both methods start at twenty-five
percent on the two development walks.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:19.079
By generation two, EvoViM
answers every question.

00:03:19.279 --> 00:03:21.903
AutoMem stays at twenty-five percent.

00:03:22.707 --> 00:03:25.017
What helped the gallery programs improve?

00:03:25.217 --> 00:03:29.896
We removed enhanced feedback and
inherited revision experience together.

00:03:30.096 --> 00:03:35.982
By generation two, all three full runs
reached seventy-five percent in development.

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:38.426
Without revision support, none did.

00:03:38.626 --> 00:03:41.525
On held-out walks, the
full programs averaged

00:03:41.525 --> 00:03:44.292
eighty-eight percent, compared with sixty.

00:03:44.492 --> 00:03:48.741
On the catalog task, the single run
without support finished ahead.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.234
The same starting tools can evolve a skill.

00:03:52.434 --> 00:03:57.182
In Tetris, the program reads the
board and projects a landing.

00:03:57.382 --> 00:04:00.482
The model moves left twice and drops.

00:04:00.682 --> 00:04:03.097
That move earns twenty-two points.

00:04:03.907 --> 00:04:08.214
The questions an agent misses today
can teach it what to keep tomorrow.

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:13.000
Explore the tools and try
EvoViM on the project page.
